---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import release from '../data/release.json';
import { releases, repository } from '../data/site.js';
const asset = (suffix) => {
  const found = release.assets.find(a => a.name.endsWith(suffix));
  if (!found) throw new Error(`Missing release asset: ${suffix}`);
  return found;
};
const platforms = [
  { name: 'Linux', arch: 'x86-64 / AMD64', mark: 'L', description: 'Choose an AppImage, a Debian package, or a portable archive.', files: [[asset('.AppImage'), 'AppImage'], [asset('.deb'), 'DEB installer'], [asset('_portable.tar.gz'), 'Portable TAR.GZ']], key: 'linux' },
  { name: 'Windows', arch: 'x64', mark: 'W', description: 'Install with the MSI, or keep a portable copy in a writable folder.', files: [[asset('.msi'), 'MSI installer'], [asset('_portable.zip'), 'Portable ZIP']], key: 'windows' },
  { name: 'macOS', arch: 'Apple Silicon / ARM64', mark: 'M', description: 'A DMG for Macs with Apple Silicon. This release has no Intel Mac download.', files: [[asset('.dmg'), 'DMG installer']], key: 'macos' },
];
---
<Layout title="Download QiRing" description="Download QiRing for Linux, Windows, and Apple Silicon Macs from the official GitHub release. Find portable builds, installation notes, and checksums.">
  <section class="shell page-intro"><p class="eyebrow">Make a Ring of your own</p><h1>At home on<br />your desktop.</h1><p class="lede">Choose a download for your computer. Every file below comes directly from the project’s GitHub release.</p><div class="release-meta"><span class="release-pill"><span class="status-dot"></span>{release.tag} · {release.prerelease ? 'Prerelease' : 'Release'}</span><span>Unsigned builds</span><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href={release.url}>Release notes ↗</a></div></section>
  <aside class="shell release-notice"><strong>A note before you install</strong><p>QiRing is early software. Try it with test data, keep independent backups, and don’t make it the only copy of important credentials. These builds are unsigned, so Windows and macOS may show trust warnings.</p></aside>
  <section class="shell download-grid" aria-label="Platform downloads">{platforms.map(platform => <article class="download-card"><div class="os-mark" aria-hidden="true">{platform.mark}</div><h2>{platform.name}</h2><span class="arch">{platform.arch}</span><p>{platform.description}</p><div class="download-options">{platform.files.map(([file,label]) => <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href={file.url} class="download-option"><span><strong>{label}</strong><small>{(file.size / 1048576).toFixed(1)} MB · {release.tag}</small></span><span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a>)}</div><div class="download-integrity"><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href={asset(`${platform.key}-SHA256SUMS`).url}>Checksums ↗</a><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href={asset(`qiring-${platform.key}.spdx.json`).url}>SBOM ↗</a></div></article>)}</section>
  <p class="shell download-footnote">Looking for a newer version or another architecture? <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href={releases}>Browse all GitHub releases ↗</a>. The files above are pinned to {release.tag}.</p>
  <section class="section shell installation" id="installation"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">From download to desktop</p><h2>A few installation notes.</h2></div><p>Verify your download, then choose the instructions for your platform.</p></div>
    <div class="faq-list">
      <details open><summary>Linux: AppImage or DEB <span aria-hidden="true">＋</span></summary><div><p>For the AppImage, make the downloaded file executable in your file manager’s properties, then launch it. In a terminal:</p><pre tabindex="0"><code>chmod +x {asset('.AppImage').name}<br />./{asset('.AppImage').name}</code></pre><p>For Debian or Ubuntu, open the DEB with your package installer, or use:</p><pre tabindex="0"><code>sudo apt install ./{asset('.deb').name}</code></pre><p>The standard AppImage and DEB use normal per-user storage. Use the separate portable archive for a portable Ring. Linux distribution compatibility can vary; <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href={`${repository}/issues`}>report your distribution and error</a> if a build won’t launch.</p></div></details>
      <details><summary>Windows: MSI installer <span aria-hidden="true">＋</span></summary><div><p>Download the x64 MSI, verify it against the published checksums, and open it to run the installer. Launch QiRing from the installed shortcut.</p><p>Because this release is unsigned, Windows may show an unknown-publisher or SmartScreen warning. Only proceed if you trust the source and have verified the download; on managed computers, follow your organization’s policy.</p><p>For a copy that keeps its Ring beside the app, choose the portable ZIP instead of the MSI.</p></div></details>
      <details><summary>macOS: Apple Silicon DMG <span aria-hidden="true">＋</span></summary><div><p>This download is for Apple Silicon Macs (M-series), not Intel Macs. Open the DMG, copy QiRing to Applications, and eject the disk image.</p><p>The app is not Developer ID signed or notarized. macOS may block the first launch. If you trust and have verified the download, consult <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/102445">Apple’s instructions for opening apps from outside the App Store</a>. Do not disable Gatekeeper globally.</p><p>macOS builds use normal per-user storage; portable mode is not supported.</p></div></details>
      <details><summary>Portable builds: keep the folder together <span aria-hidden="true">＋</span></summary><div><p>Extract the Linux TAR.GZ or Windows ZIP into a folder you own and can write to. Keep the <code>qiring-portable</code> marker beside the AppImage or Windows executable.</p><p>QiRing creates a <code>QiRingData</code> directory beside the launcher. Move that directory, the launcher, and the marker together. Keep independent backups before moving between computers.</p><a href="/guide/storage/">Read the complete portable-storage guide →</a></div></details>
      <details><summary>Checksums, SBOMs, and provenance <span aria-hidden="true">＋</span></summary><div><p>Each platform has a SHA-256 checksum file and an SPDX software bill of materials (SBOM, an inventory of dependencies). To check a whole platform manifest, download all its files—including the SBOM—into one folder.</p><p>On Linux:</p><pre tabindex="0"><code>sha256sum --check linux-SHA256SUMS</code></pre><p>On macOS:</p><pre tabindex="0"><code>shasum -a 256 -c macos-SHA256SUMS</code></pre><p>On Windows, compare the result of this command to the matching entry in <code>windows-SHA256SUMS</code>:</p><pre tabindex="0"><code>Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 .\{asset('.msi').name}</code></pre><p>Checksums detect file differences; they do not replace a trusted download source or a platform signature. With the GitHub CLI, you can also check build provenance:</p><pre tabindex="0"><code>gh attestation verify &lt;downloaded-file&gt; --repo gcoxdev/qiring</code></pre></div></details>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="shell cta-panel"><div><p class="eyebrow">Installed? Start here.</p><h2>Your first Ring awaits.</h2><p>Learn the vocabulary, create a Ring, and save your recovery key before adding your essentials.</p></div><a class="button" href="/guide/getting-started/">Open the getting-started guide ↗</a></section>
</Layout>
